\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Clock and shift}
\date{2026-11-03}


\p{Let #{N\ge 1} and let #{R} be a commutative unital ring containing
an element #{\omega} with #{\omega^N=1}. On
#{\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z} set #{\chi(i)=\omega^i}, let
#{U=M_\chi} be the [multiplication operator](fdip-00AI) of #{\chi},
and let #{T} be the [cyclic shift](fdip-00AN). \ref{fdip-0013} gives
#{TU=M_{T\chi}T}. Since
#{\,(T\chi)(i)=\chi(i+1)=\omega^{i+1}=\omega\chi(i)}, one has
#{M_{T\chi}=\omega U}, and therefore #{TU=\omega UT}. When
#{\omega\ne 1} the two products differ. Also #{T^N=\bbI} and
#{U^N=M_{\chi^N}=\bbI}. If #{\omega} is primitive of order #{N}, the
operators #{T}, #{U} and the powers of #{\omega} generate a finite
Heisenberg group. The four-cycle is the case #{N=4}.}

\p{The continuum form is the commutator of differentiation with
multiplication by the coordinate. For a smooth function #{f},
##{\frac{d}{dx}(xf)-x\frac{d}{dx}f=f,}
so #{\bigl[\tfrac{d}{dx},x\bigr]=1}. With #{p=-\ui\hbar\,d/dx} this is
#{[p,x]=-\ui\hbar}.}

\p{The same relation holds over the field #{\mathbb F_p} when #{N}
divides #{p-1}. Then #{X^N-1} divides
#{X^{p-1}-1=\prod_{c\in\mathbb F_p^\times}(X-c)}. The divisor is
separable, because #{p} does not divide #{N} and #{X^N-1} shares no
root with its derivative, so it has #{N} distinct roots in
#{\mathbb F_p}. Those roots form a subgroup #{G}. Let #{m} be the
least common multiple of the orders of its elements, and factor #{m}
into prime powers. For each prime power #{q} in that factorization,
some element of #{G} has order divisible by #{q}, and a power of it
has order #{q}. The product of one such element for each prime power
has order #{m}: the factors commute, and their orders are coprime.
Every element of #{G} is a root of #{X^m-1}, and a field has at most
#{m} roots of that polynomial, so #{N\le m}. Orders in #{G} divide
#{N}, so #{m} divides #{N}. Hence #{m=N}, and #{G} contains a
primitive #{N}-th root.}
